---
// How it works. Copy: advisory_based_specs/02_Replacement_Copy.md §3.
// The advisory process comes first; the public research rules follow.
// Every fragment ID from the old methodology page is kept so older links
// still land on the matching section: evidence-categories, independence,
// strength-levels, confidence-tiers, contradictions, external-validation,
// exclusions, problem-vs-payment, limitations, privacy, decision-criteria.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Kicker from '../components/content/Kicker.astro';

const title = 'How I work with founders | PainToBuild';
const description = 'I start with your decision, review what you know and compare the options. I suggest a test, then review what happened and what you might do next.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How I work with you',
  description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PainToBuild' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogImagePath="/open-graph/methodology.png">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <article class="wrap section prose">
    <Kicker>How it works</Kicker>
    <h1>How I work with you</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I start by understanding the decision you need to make. The research and review then focus
      on what would help you make that decision.
    </p>

    <h2>Understand the question</h2>
    <p>
      I ask what you are deciding, why it matters now and what you have tried. I also ask about
      your time and budget. Each review covers one decision so the work stays manageable.
    </p>

    <h2 id="evidence-categories">Review the information</h2>
    <p>
      I may review your product, customer comments, usage numbers or past efforts to find
      customers. The agreed work may also include looking at other products and public discussions.
    </p>
    <p>I make clear which details you supplied, which I checked and which are my interpretation.</p>

    <h2>Consider other explanations</h2>
    <p>
      There may be several reasons something is not working. I look for information that supports
      each explanation and information that goes against it. I also consider whether the product
      solves a problem people care enough about.
    </p>

    <h2 id="decision-criteria">Choose a test</h2>
    <p>
      I recommend a test that fits your time and budget. The review explains what to do, what to
      watch for and how the result could change the advice.
    </p>
    <p>If more research is needed first, I explain what to find out.</p>

    <h2>Review the results</h2>
    <p>
      After you run the test, I look at what happened. I check whether the result answers the
      original question and explain what I would do next.
    </p>
    <p>
      A test that was not run tells you something different from a test that people rejected.
      Missing or unreliable numbers may mean the result is still unclear.
    </p>

    <h2 id="independence">How I use public research</h2>
    <p>
      Public posts can point to problems worth looking into. I avoid counting the same person's
      story several times. Different accounts may belong to one person, and people may repeat
      something they read elsewhere. When that is unclear, I note it.
    </p>
    <p>Several people describing a problem can be a reason to investigate. It does not prove that your product will sell.</p>

    <h3 id="strength-levels">Which reports are useful?</h3>
    <p>
      A detailed account can tell you what happened, what the person tried and why it mattered. A
      short expression of interest usually tells you less.
    </p>
    <p>The important question is whether the report helps answer the question you are researching.</p>

    <h3 id="confidence-tiers">What the research shows</h3>
    <p>
      I separate what people reported from possible explanations and unanswered questions. I use
      counts only when I can explain what was counted and show the records behind them.
    </p>

    <h3 id="contradictions">What people already use</h3>
    <p>
      People may already solve the problem with another product, a spreadsheet or help from
      someone else. They may also choose to live with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I look at these options before suggesting that a new product is needed. That includes
      checking whether an ordinary AI tool can do the job well enough.
    </p>

    <h3 id="external-validation">What payment claims mean</h3>
    <p>A price on a website shows that something is for sale. It does not show that anyone bought it.</p>
    <p>
      Someone saying they might pay shows interest. A report of a purchase tells you more, but it
      is still a report unless checked.
    </p>
    <p>A confirmed payment shows that one purchase happened. It does not prove that other people will buy your offer.</p>

    <h3 id="exclusions">What I leave out of the count</h3>
    <p>I do not count repeated posts, general advice or sales claims as separate reports of customer need.</p>
    <p>
      A comment can still be useful if the writer describes their own problem. It does not have to
      be the first post in a discussion.
    </p>

    <h3 id="problem-vs-payment">A problem is not proof of a sale</h3>
    <p>
      You still need to find out who can buy, how to reach them and why they would choose your
      product. You also need to understand the cost of finding and serving those customers.
    </p>

    <h3 id="limitations">What the research cannot tell you</h3>
    <p>
      People who post online do not represent everyone. Their accounts may leave out important
      details. Information about competing products can go out of date. Small tests can also give
      misleading results.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some older guides use a worksheet that scores ideas in twelve areas. It helps organize the
      research. The score does not decide whether a business will work or what advice I give a client.
    </p>

    <h3 id="privacy">Sources and private information</h3>
    <p>
      Research articles include relevant links and dates. A source link may reveal the writer's
      name even if it is not shown on my page.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before you share private business information, I explain how it will be handled. The
      privacy page describes those arrangements.
    </p>

    <ul class="link-list end-links">
      <li><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a></li>
      <li><a href="/evidence/">Research articles</a></li>
      <li><a href="/example-review/">Example review</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/advisory/"
         data-event="advisory_cta_click" data-event-page="/methodology/" data-event-cta-id="methodology-end">
        See how I can help
      </a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section { padding-block: var(--s7) var(--s8); }
  h2[id], h3[id] { scroll-margin-top: var(--s5); }
  .end-links { margin-top: var(--s7); padding-top: var(--s5); border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
</style>
